  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>How Solar Panel Payback Period &amp; 25-Year ROI Are Calculated</h2>
    <p>
      Contractors and energy analysts evaluate rooftop solar using both <strong>simple payback</strong> (ignoring future rate hikes) and <strong>inflated cash-flow payback</strong> (accounting for annual utility escalation and panel degradation). Both metrics start with the net installed capital outlay after upfront rebates or qualifying commercial credits.
    </p>
    <div class="ac-formula">
Gross System Cost ($)   = System Size (kW) × 1,000 × Installed Cost ($/W)
Net System Cost ($)     = Gross Cost − Federal Credit (if eligible) − State/Utility Rebate
Year-1 Output (kWh)     = System Size (kW) × Annual Yield (kWh/kW/yr)
Year-1 Savings ($)      = Year-1 Output × Electric Rate ($/kWh) × Effective Bill Offset (%)
Simple Payback (Years)  = Net System Cost ÷ Year-1 Savings

Year-t Savings ($)      = Year-1 Output × (1 − Degradation)^(t−1) × Electric Rate × (1 + Escalation)^(t−1) × Offset%
25-Year Net Profit ($)  = Σ(Year 1 to 25 Savings) − Net System Cost − Inverter/O&amp;M Reserve
25-Year ROI (%)         = (25-Year Net Profit ÷ Net System Cost) × 100
    </div>
    <h3>Key Trade &amp; Financial Assumptions Used by US Estimators</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Installed Cost per Watt ($/W):</strong> Cash turnkey quotes in 2026 typically fall between <strong>$2.50 and $3.50 per DC watt</strong> before rebates, depending on roof pitch, electrical main-panel upgrades (200A service swaps add $2,500–$4,500), and local permitting fees.</li>
      <li><strong>Effective Bill Offset (Net Metering):</strong> Utilities with full 1:1 retail net metering credit exported kilowatt-hours at your retail rate (~95–100% effective value). States with net billing or avoided-cost export tariffs (such as California NEM 3.0 without a battery) reduce effective bill offset to roughly 55–75% unless paired with storage.</li>
      <li><strong>Inverter &amp; Maintenance Reserve:</strong> Tier-1 monocrystalline modules carry 25-year performance warranties (typically 84–88% output at year 25), whereas string inverters usually need replacement once around years 10–14 ($1,800–$3,500). Microinverters carry 25-year warranties but cost more upfront.</li>
      <li><strong>Financing Dealer Fees:</strong> Low-APR solar loans (e.g., 3.99%–5.99%) frequently embed 15%–30% dealer origination fees into the principal. Always compare cash price versus financed principal before calculating payback.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

