  <section class="ac-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Solar Payback in 2026</h2>
    <div class="ac-faq-list">
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>1. Is the 30% federal residential solar tax credit still available in 2026?</h3>
        <p>No. The homeowner-direct 30% Residential Clean Energy Credit under IRC Section 25D expired for residential solar and battery installations placed in service after December 31, 2025. Commercial installations and third-party-owned (TPO) solar leases/PPAs may still utilize the commercial Section 48E tax credit, which providers can partially pass through via lower lease or PPA pricing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>2. What is a realistic solar panel payback period in the US in 2026?</h3>
        <p>For a cash-purchased residential system in 2026, realistic payback ranges from <strong>7 to 10 years</strong> in high-rate states (California, Massachusetts, Connecticut, New York) and <strong>11 to 16 years</strong> in moderate-rate Midwest and Southern states ($0.14–$0.17/kWh). In low-rate hydroelectric states ($0.10–$0.12/kWh), payback often exceeds 16 years without state rebates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>3. How much does net metering affect my solar ROI?</h3>
        <p>Net metering is often the single biggest factor after your retail electricity rate. Under full 1:1 net metering, every kWh sent to the grid offsets a kWh bought at night. Under net billing tariffs (where exports are credited at wholesale rates of $0.04–$0.08/kWh), payback can lengthen by 3 to 6 years unless you self-consume daytime solar or install battery storage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>4. Should I replace my roof before installing solar panels?</h3>
        <p>Yes, if your asphalt shingle roof has fewer than 10–12 years of remaining life. Removing and reinstalling a solar array mid-life for a roof tear-off typically costs <strong>$3,000 to $6,500</strong> in crane and electrician labor, which can erase 2 to 4 years of solar savings.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ac-faq-item">
        <h3>5. Why do low-interest solar loans often have a worse ROI than cash?</h3>
        <p>Many 25-year solar loans advertised at 3.99%–5.99% APR include hidden dealer fees of 18%–30% added directly to the loan balance. A $24,000 cash quote can balloon to $31,000 financed. Always request the installer's cash price and compare it against a standard home equity line or credit union loan with zero dealer fee.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
